Step 1: Check which image is configured

Run:

Shell
kubectl describe deployment myapp
 

or

Shell
kubectl get deployment myapp -o yaml | grep image

Example output:

Plain Text
image: hyarel78/java-webapp:v2
Step 2: Check the exact error

Run:

Shell
kubectl describe pod myapp-664896fcf4-2rlbz

Look at the Events section at the bottom.

Common errors:

Plain Text
pull access denied

or

Plain Text
repository does not exist

or

Plain Text
manifest unknown
Step 3: Verify values.yaml

Open:

Shell
nano values.yaml
 

Look for:

YAML
image:
repository: hyarel78/java-webapp
tag: "v1"
pullPolicy: IfNotPresent

Make sure:

Repository name is correct
Tag exists in Docker Hub
Step 4: Test image manually

On the VM:

Shell
docker pull hyarel78/java-webapp:v1

If this fails:

Plain Text
pull access denied

then the image is:

not pushed to Docker Hub
private
wrong repository name
wrong tag
Step 5: Push image to Docker Hub

If image is local only:

Shell
docker login
Shell
docker images
Shell
docker tag java-webapp:v1 hyarel78/java-webapp:v1
Shell
docker push hyarel78/java-webapp:v1
Step 6: Upgrade Helm

After correcting values.yaml:

Shell
helm upgrade myapp ./myapp

Check rollout:

Shell
kubectl rollout restart deployment myapp

Verify:

Shell
kubectl get pods
Most useful command right now

Run:

Shell
kubectl describe pod myapp-664896fcf4-2rlbz

and share the Events section at the bottom. That will show the exact reason for the ErrImagePull error.
